We present RADAR (Robust Adversarial-Resistant Detection with Adaptive
Reasoning), a hybrid neural system for binary AI-generated text detection
submitted to the PAN 2026 Voight-Kampff shared task. The system fuses deep
semantic representations extracted by a large pretrained encoder with
handcrafted stylometric signals through a cross-attention mechanism, yielding
a unified representation for classification. RADAR was trained in two stages:
first with the encoder frozen to stabilize the fusion components, then with
all weights jointly fine-tuned. Training combined the official PAN-2026 task
data with a curated subset of the RAID benchmark to improve robustness against
adversarial obfuscations. On the PAN-2026 official validation set, the final
model achieves a mean score of 0.997 across all five evaluation metrics
(ROC-AUC, Brier, C@1, F1, F0.5u), substantially outperforming the provided
baselines and demonstrating strong generalization across genres and generator
types.
